  "query_preview": "..\nFriday night through Monday: The Inland Northwest is currently \nunder a deep southerly flow as a large upper-level low and \noccluded front approaches the northwest coast. This low will \nweaken as it moves inland and the base of the trough dives into \nnorthern California. Weak synoptic ascent associated with the \noccluded front will generate a band of light precipitation \ntonight into Saturday morning. Precipitation totals will be \nmaximized over the Cascades, Palouse, Camas Prairie, and the \ncentral Idaho Panhandle with amounts up to 0.40 inches. \n\nClearing in the wake of this precipitation will promote low \nstratus and fog development early Saturday morning, particularly\nwithin sheltered valleys, the Palouse, and the Spokane area. \nWeather dries out briefly during the day on Saturday with \nafternoon temperatures cooling by 5-10 degrees. A post-frontal \ncold pool aloft (-20C to - 23C) will steepen mid-level lapse \nrates, supporting isolated light showers across the Cascades, \nfar northeast Washington, and north Idaho. Winds will be breezy \nout of the south Saturday afternoon with gusts 15-25 mph across \nthe Basin into the Spokane area and Palouse. \n\nActive weather continues late Saturday night into Sunday with \nanother frontal system approaching the northwest coast. The \nleading warm front and surface south to southeast flow will \nsupport light rain across the east slopes of the Cascades and \nthe western Columbia Basin early Sunday morning before the \ntrailing cold front brings a return of downsloping flow off the \nCascades by the mid to late morning. Stronger jet dynamics \nassociated with this front will provide better forcing to the \nInland Northwest as it moves into far eastern Washington and \nnorth Idaho on Sunday. The front will slow down through the day,\nprolonging precipitation over eastern Washington and north \nIdaho through early Monday morning and resulting in higher \nprecipitation totals than the Friday system. NBM rainfall \namounts show up to 0.15 inches in the immediate lee of the \nCascades and 0.10 to 0.60 inches across the Cascades, far \neastern Washington and north Idaho. Snow levels across the \nCascades, northeast Washington and far north Idaho will be \naround 5000 to 5500 feet Saturday night through Sunday afternoon\nwith snow amounts 2 to 5 inches above pass level and 1 to 4 \ninches at Washington and Sherman Pass level. \n\nTuesday through Thursday: A brief lull in the active weather is\nexpected on Tuesday though it will be chilly. Light winds and \nresidual boundary layer moisture will favor fog and stratus \ndevelopment across much of the region.\n\nConfidence in the forecast decreases for the Wednesday and \nThursday timeframe as the next upper-level low approaches the \nregion. While models are in solid agreement on the presence of \nthis system and for it to move inland, there remains solid \ndifferences on the exact track. Around 51% of global ensembles \nshow the center of this low diving into Oregon and northern \nCalifornia, leaving the Inland Northwest on the drier side of \nthis low. The other 49% have the track farther north across \nWashington and Oregon, which would be the scenario to monitor \nfor mountain pass snow and lowland snow/rain. /vmt",
